Our markdown rendering is often hard to read. We should aim
for a more complete markdown->TUI (or console)
implementation that takes into account all the common
markdown idioms that apply to text rendering. A few
examples:

We could remove markers like these in the rendering output.
Links could be rendered more simply.

It also appears that long styled strings don't get properly
styled across lines. Perhaps this can be simplified by
formalizing our notion of a paragraph to include lines with
breaks.

It would be helpful if lists:

• Rendered like this when their text wraps on long lines.
  ◦ As well as for nested bullets that also have long lines.
    Otherwise the text gets really hard to read.

1. And the same with bulleted lists that also have very long
   lines that would otherwise wrap to the first column.
2. Like this, which is very ugly and difficult to read in
   large lists.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

HRs should really render as actual horizontal rules and not
just three hyphens.

▎ Much liks lists, it would be great if comments also
▎ rendered and wrapped in such a way that the indendentation
▎ was clear.

▎ Another case:
▎
▎ It would also be nice if comment blocks rendered properly
▎ with nested markup, so blocks like this don't turn into an
▎ unending sea of gray.
▎
▎ # with unformatted code
▎
▎ And lists that look like
▎
▎ • still
▎ • more
▎ • gray

We also need different

Treatment for each kind

of header

It would also be nice if long lines rendered naturally as a
paragraph when they wrap past the last column in the
viewport. But it would also be nice if paragraphs that had
been auto-wrapped by an editor or an agent that got
overzealous about formatting everything fastidiously.

I believe it's the markdown norm to only consider paragraph
breaks with a newline. But that's also worth
double-checking.

